LightGBM and Random Forest have the highest accuracy
difference between the clean symptoms accuracy and
corrupted symptoms accuracy. This means that they
don't perform well on data that is different from the
one that they were trained on.

The averages are : Logistic Regression, Multinomial
Naive Bayes and Gaussian Naive Bayes.

The machines who did not regress as soon as the data
got tricky and new are Bernoulli Naive Bayes (surprisingly)
and K-Nearest Neighbor.

Finding the single flip fraction to be rigid and unfair,
I tried out fractions lesser than 15% so as to give the
underperforming machines a sense of justice.

Random forest stayed in the range of 0.90 when the data
was corrupted only by 2% and 5%. But as soon as the data
was corrupted by 10% and 15%, it degraded hard.

LightBoost did even worse than the ensemble.

flip_frac              0.02     0.05     0.10    0.15
Logistic Regression	: 0.9996	0.9927	0.9645	0.8970

The average LR kept 0.9 in all corrupted levels.
Gaussian Naive Bayes did the same too.
Multinomial isn't bad either.

Bernoulli and KNN kept all of their accuracies strictly
at 0.90.

Conclusion : Bernoulli and KNN are the winners of
dataset testing.